{"h": {"measure": "P", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 129, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 1->3 is none and edge 1->2 is none, node 1 has no outgoing path, so adding a conv on edge 0 is wasted; with e4 present the conv feeds the output directly."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "skip_connect", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing the input-to-output skip connection destroys the residual path that deep stacked cells rely on, hurting more than removing a conv on that edge."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a residual shortcut helps most in conv-heavy cells that are otherwise hard to optimize, and helps little in parameter-free all-skip or all-pool cells."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a conv where there was no edge both adds capacity and connects node 2 to the output, whereas replacing an existing skip or pool adds less."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing a 3x3 conv on the last edge with a skip removes the main learned transformation into the output, which hurts more than replacing other ops."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without the 0->3 skip, the remaining paths to the output carry more of the signal, so deleting edge 1->3 hurts more."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "In an otherwise empty cell, edge 3 is the only path, so a conv there adds essential capacity; in conv-rich cells, swapping the skip for a conv removes the useful residual."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", 